{
  "title": "Oliver Twist: A Literary Discussion",
  "segments": [
    {
      "title": "The Workhouse and the Weight of Systems",
      "segment_type": "opening",
      "turns": [
        {
          "speaker": "Host",
          "role": "host",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "Welcome to Oliver Twist Unpacked, a close reading of Dickens's second novel \u2014 a book about a child who has almost no power and almost no voice.",
              "sentence_type": "intro",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.98,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "power",
                "voice"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "Today we're asking what the novel actually believes about systems \u2014 workhouses, courts, criminal gangs \u2014 and whether anyone escapes them or simply gets lucky.",
              "sentence_type": "intro",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.98,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 500,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "systems",
                "lucky"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "With me are three people I trust to make that argument properly.",
              "sentence_type": "intro",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.98,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "Edmund Leigh is a retired Oxford moral philosopher who reads fiction as a laboratory for practical ethics \u2014 virtue under pressure, and where weakness shades into wickedness.",
              "sentence_type": "intro",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.97,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "virtue",
                "wickedness"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "Daniel Rosen is a cultural historian who asks who owns what, whose labour is made invisible, and what economic forces determine a character's options \u2014 with precise historical data to back it up.",
              "sentence_type": "intro",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.98,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "labour",
                "economic"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "And Oliver Trevelyan is an actor and the voice of more classic audiobooks than anyone alive \u2014 he reads what silent reading misses, the breath patterns, the rhythms that only make sense as speech.",
              "sentence_type": "intro",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.99,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 800,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "breath",
                "speech"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "Daniel, I want to start with you \u2014 you've described Fagin as a more rational employer than the workhouse itself.",
              "sentence_type": "question",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.98,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "rational",
                "workhouse"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "But if Fagin is a symptom of the system rather than its villain, what does it mean that Dickens's solution \u2014 Brownlow, the country house \u2014 is just as extra-systemic as Fagin?",
              "sentence_type": "question",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.97,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1200,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "symptom",
                "extra-systemic"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Daniel Rosen",
          "role": "marxist_cultural_historian",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "Fagin extends credit, organises shelter and training, and fills the vacuum the 1834 Poor Law deliberately created by making workhouse relief deliberately intolerable.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.99,
              "pause_before_ms": 220,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "vacuum",
                "intolerable"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c10:p14"
            },
            {
              "text": "Brownlow does the same thing from the other end of the class ladder \u2014 he removes Oliver from the contradiction entirely rather than resolving it.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.99,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 500,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "removes",
                "contradiction"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p43"
            },
            {
              "text": "Dickens is essentially agreeing that the system cannot be reformed \u2014 he just doesn't say so out loud.",
              "sentence_type": "punchline",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 1.01,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1200,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "reformed"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Edmund Leigh",
          "role": "moral_philosopher",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "I think Daniel is right on the structural point, though I arrive there differently.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.94,
              "pause_before_ms": 200,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "Every genuine rescue in this novel is enacted by a private individual exercising personal moral judgement \u2014 not a reformed institution.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.94,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "private",
                "moral"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p43"
            },
            {
              "text": "When Brownlow says of Oliver, with what Dickens calls 'great energy,' 'I really fear that he is ill' \u2014 that is an act of individual moral attention, and it saves the boy.",
              "sentence_type": "quote_setup",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "setup",
              "rate": 0.94,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 150,
              "emphasis_words": [],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p43"
            },
            {
              "text": "'I really fear that he is ill.'",
              "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
              "is_quote": true,
              "quote_mode": "reading",
              "rate": 0.93,
              "pause_before_ms": 150,
              "pause_after_ms": 400,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "fear",
                "ill"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p43"
            },
            {
              "text": "No magistrate, no Poor Law guardian says those words \u2014 only one private gentleman who happens to be paying attention.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "commentary",
              "rate": 1.0,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1200,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "private",
                "attention"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p43"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Oliver Trevelyan",
          "role": "actor_and_narrator",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "What strikes me is what happens just before Brownlow speaks \u2014 Oliver loses language completely.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 1.02,
              "pause_before_ms": 210,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "loses",
                "language"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p45"
            },
            {
              "text": "Dickens gives us: 'Oliver tried to reply but his tongue failed him. He was deadly pale; and the whole place seemed turning round and round.'",
              "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
              "is_quote": true,
              "quote_mode": "reading",
              "rate": 0.93,
              "pause_before_ms": 150,
              "pause_after_ms": 400,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "tongue",
                "failed",
                "turning"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p45"
            },
            {
              "text": "The syntax enacts the collapse \u2014 subject, verb, then the world itself losing orientation.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "commentary",
              "rate": 1.0,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "enacts",
                "collapse"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p45"
            },
            {
              "text": "And I think this is where Edmund's moral architecture and Daniel's structural critique both run into trouble \u2014 because this technique bypasses conscious judgement entirely.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 1.0,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "bypasses",
                "judgement"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p45"
            },
            {
              "text": "Dickens doesn't argue you into sympathy \u2014 he puts the dizziness in your chest before your mind has had a vote.",
              "sentence_type": "punchline",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 1.02,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1200,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "dizziness",
                "vote"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c11:p45"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Edmund Leigh",
          "role": "moral_philosopher",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "That is beautifully observed \u2014 and slightly alarming, if I may say so.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.94,
              "pause_before_ms": 230,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "alarming"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "A moral argument that bypasses moral reasoning is closer to propaganda than to ethics.",
              "sentence_type": "punchline",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.93,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1200,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "propaganda",
                "ethics"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Daniel Rosen",
          "role": "marxist_cultural_historian",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "Though propaganda can tell the truth \u2014 the chase scene proves it.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 1.01,
              "pause_before_ms": 240,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "truth"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c10:p21"
            },
            {
              "text": "'One wretched breathless child, panting with exhaustion; terror in his looks; agony in his eyes' \u2014 and the crowd hails his weakness with joy.",
              "sentence_type": "quote_reading",
              "is_quote": true,
              "quote_mode": "reading",
              "rate": 0.93,
              "pause_before_ms": 150,
              "pause_after_ms": 400,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "wretched",
                "joy"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c10:p21"
            },
            {
              "text": "That crowd isn't evil \u2014 they're performing the only social function the system has taught them: surveillance of the poor.",
              "sentence_type": "analysis",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "commentary",
              "rate": 0.99,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1200,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "surveillance",
                "poor"
              ],
              "passage_ref": "c10:p21"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Host",
          "role": "host",
          "utterances": [
            {
              "text": "So all three of you land in roughly the same place \u2014 the system cannot be reformed from within \u2014 but the routes are entirely different.",
              "sentence_type": "transition",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.98,
              "pause_before_ms": 260,
              "pause_after_ms": 300,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "reformed"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "In our next segment, we'll look at Fagin's world more closely \u2014 and ask whether that underground economy is a mirror or a distortion of the world above it.",
              "sentence_type": "closing",
              "is_quote": false,
              "quote_mode": "none",
              "rate": 0.97,
              "pause_before_ms": 0,
              "pause_after_ms": 1500,
              "emphasis_words": [
                "mirror",
                "distortion"
              ],
              "passage_ref": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
